A presidential election was held in Portugal on 14 January 1996.

Incumbent president [[Mário Soares]] was constitutionally barred from a third consecutive term. The [[Social Democratic Party (Portugal)|Social Democrats]] were coming from a clear defeat in the [[1995 Portuguese legislative election]], and their former leader, [[Aníbal Cavaco Silva]], who had left the office of Prime Minister after 10 years at the helm, ran against the Mayor of [[Lisbon]], [[Jorge Sampaio]]. This was a rematch of the [[1991 Portuguese legislative election|1991 legislative election]], where Cavaco Silva defeated Jorge Sampaio by a 51 to 29 percent margin.

The other left-wing candidates, [[Jerónimo de Sousa]] and Alberto Matos, presented by the [[Portuguese Communist Party]] and the [[People's Democratic Union (Portugal)|People's Democratic Union]] respectively, both withdrew from the race one week before the elections, announcing their support for [[Jorge Sampaio]], in order to concentrate the left-wing vote and avoid a [[Two-round system|runoff]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://arquivos.rtp.pt/conteudos/jeronimo-de-sousa-desiste-da-candidatura/|title=Jerónimo de Sousa desiste da candidatura |language=pt |work=RTP |date=8 January 1996|access-date=15 January 2025}}</ref> These parties had already supported Sampaio in a coalition that won the local elections in Lisbon. It would be the last time that People's Democratic Union presented a candidate, as two years later it merged with other small left-wing parties and formed the [[Left Bloc (Portugal)|Left Bloc]].

Cavaco Silva was supported by the [[Social Democratic Party (Portugal)|Social Democratic Party]], failing to get the support of the [[CDS – People's Party|People's Party]], led by [[Manuel Monteiro]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lourenço |first=Eunice |date=2004-03-08 |title=Como Paulo Portas apoiou Cavaco em 1996 |url=https://www.publico.pt/2004/03/08/jornal/como-paulo-portas-apoiou-cavaco-em-1996-185207 |access-date=2025-06-19 |website=PÚBLICO |language=pt}}</ref> On election day, Sampaio won with nearly 54 percent of the votes and by a more than 400,000 vote margin over Cavaco Silva, which gathered just over 46 percent of the votes and, once more, the right-wing parties did not win the presidential election.

Sampaio gained the majority of the votes in all the districts in the South of Portugal, including the Communist strongholds in [[Alentejo]] and [[Setúbal District|Setúbal district]]. Cavaco won in the more conservative districts of the North (excluding [[Porto District|Porto district]], where Sampaio edged out Cavaco by a narrow 52 to 48 percent margin) and also in [[Leiria District|Leiria district]], traditional strongholds of the right-wing parties.

[[Voter turnout]] increased to 66% of registered voters compared to the previous presidential election, although it stood basically at the same level as the 1995 legislative election, held just three months earlier. Sampaio was sworn in as President on 9 March 1996.<ref>{{Cite web |date=1996-03-09 |title=Jorge Sampaio toma posse como Presidente da República |url=https://arquivos.rtp.pt/conteudos/jorge-sampaio-toma-posse-como-presidente-da-republica/ |access-date=2026-02-18 |website=RTP Arquivos |language=pt}}</ref> Aníbal Cavaco Silva would have to wait ten more years to be elected president in [[2006 Portuguese presidential election|2006]].

==Background==
Mário Soares was re-elected by a landslide with 70% of the votes in January 1991.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-01-18 |title=Presidenciais. Mário Soares foi o presidente eleito com maior percentagem de votos desde 1976 |url=https://observador.pt/2026/01/18/presidenciais-mario-soares-foi-o-presidente-eleito-com-maior-percentagem-de-votos-desde-1976/ |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=Observador |language=pt}}</ref> In contrast to his first term, Soares' second term revealed a very tense relationship with prime minister [[Aníbal Cavaco Silva]] of the PSD.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2017-01-07 |title=Soares, uma vida (parte 5): o Presidente popular que irritava os governantes |url=https://expresso.pt/politica/2017-01-07-Soares-uma-vida--parte-5--o-Presidente-popular-que-irritava-os-governantes |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=Expresso |language=pt}}</ref> Soares' "open presidencies", where he would visit areas across the country to listen to citizens concerns and learn about their problems, were used by the President to criticize Cavaco's government,<ref>{{Cite web |date=2005-10-20 |title=Os anos de co-habitação pouco pacífica de Soares e Cavaco |url=https://www.rtp.pt/noticias/pais/os-anos-de-co-habitacao-pouco-pacifica-de-soares-e-cavaco_n19157 |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=RTP |language=pt}}</ref> adding to this press leaks, in which the presidency appeared to be undermining the government.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2016-01-09 |title=Quando Soares dissolveu Cavaco à mesa do Aviz |url=https://expresso.pt/presidenciais2016/2016-01-09-Quando-Soares-dissolveu-Cavaco-a-mesa-do-Aviz |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=Expresso |language=pt}}</ref> 

===Pre-campaign===

In the follow-up for the presidential election, [[Mayor of Lisbon|Lisbon mayor]] and former PS leader [[Jorge Sampaio]] launched his campaign early on, in February 1995,<ref>{{Cite web |date=1995-02-07 |title=Jorge Sampaio candidato à Presidência da República |url=https://arquivos.rtp.pt/conteudos/jorge-sampaio-candidato-a-presidencia-da-republica/ |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=RTP |language=pt}}</ref> an announcement that surprised and constrained then party leader [[António Guterres]], with President Soares also having reservations about Sampaio, although both would later strongly support him.<ref name=":JSFG95" /> Former president [[António Ramalho Eanes]] was rumored to be considering a run, which he ultimately dismissed,<ref name=":ARE96" /> while in the PSD, although some considered the possibility of running, such as former prime minister [[Francisco Pinto Balsemão]],<ref name=":FPB96">{{Cite web |date=2021-09-02 |title=Presidenciais de 1996, a última "tentação" de Pinto Balsemão |url=https://www.rtp.pt/noticias/cultura/presidenciais-de-1996-a-ultima-tentacao-de-pinto-balsemao_n1346103 |access-date=2026-08-28 |website=RTP |language=pt}}</ref> being even "sounded out" by the Socialist Party in view of Guterres' initial reluctance to support Sampaio,<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-09-04 |title=As memórias de Balsemão, o político que escolheu o jornalismo |url=https://rr.pt/especial/politica/2021/09/04/as-memorias-de-balsemao-o-politico-que-escolheu-o-jornalismo/251973/ |access-date=2026-08-28 |website=Renascença |language=pt}}</ref> the candidacy of prime minister Cavaco Silva was always strongly speculated upon, with himself entertaining the idea,<ref>{{Cite web |date=2014-03-08 |title=Cavaco, 36 anos depois |url=https://expresso.pt/blogues/blogue_chamem_me_o_que_quiserem/2016-03-08-Cavaco-36-anos-depois |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=Expresso |language=pt}}</ref> and being made official a few days after the 1995 legislative election.<ref>{{Cite web |date=1995-10-10 |title=Anúncio de candidatura presidencial de Cavaco Silva |url=https://arquivos.rtp.pt/conteudos/anuncio-de-candidatura-presidencial-de-cavaco-silva/ |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=RTP |language=pt}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
Any [[Portuguese nationality law|Portuguese citizen]] over 35 years old has the opportunity to run for president. In order to do so it is necessary to gather between 7,500 and 15,000 signatures and submit them to the [[Portuguese Constitutional Court]].

According to the [[Constitution of Portugal|Portuguese Constitution]], to be elected, a candidate needs a majority of votes. If no candidate gets this majority there will take place a second round between the two most voted candidates.

